{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UHsFY84u8tPrgvCHkYtt1k","type":"tool_result","content":"120\t\\end{table}\n121\t\n122\t\\subsection{Cases, seeds and reported uncertainty}\n123\tEach table contributes two cases, a top pair and a mid pair, giving four held-out cases (crowd: F1 top, F2 mid; sports: F3 top, F4 mid) plus two permutation controls (F5, F6) on the top pairs. The held-out evaluation ran two seeded episodes (seeds 21 and 22); the development evaluation ran two (seeds 11 and 12). A family score is the mean of the case scores within a design, and the overall score is the mean of the two family scores. Uncertainty is limited by design: there are two episodes, B1 has 20 random draws per case, and the two held-out episodes returned identical per-case budgets. We therefore report no confidence intervals or significance tests, and nothing here is a population-level interval over data sets.\n124\t\n125\t\\subsection{Evaluation scopes and qualification}\n126\tSearch configurations were scored on the development tables, and one frozen configuration was then evaluated in a single sealed held-out evaluation. We keep the two scopes in separate tables and sections and never combine their scores. No search configuration exceeded the starting baseline on the development tables (Section~\\ref{sec:dev}), so the frozen selection is not a winner; it is one of three configurations with identical development scores. Its held-out evaluation completed and was recorded as qualified, with a true success indicator. The evidence does not record which gate criteria the qualification decision covers, so we read it only as a statement that the sealed evaluation ran to completion and was recorded as qualified; we do not read it as certifying controls or validity. The control outcomes in Section~\\ref{sec:controls} are separate observations taken from the recorded episodes. Qualification is not evidence of superiority over the starting baseline, which was not run on the held-out tables, and it does not extend the findings beyond the declared cases.\n127\t\n128\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n129\t\\centering\n130\t\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/protocol_overview.png}\n131\t\\caption{Protocol overview. A comparison table is fit with a ridge-penalised Bradley--Terry model; a target adjacent pair (top or mid) is selected; a removal search proposes at most $2\\%$ of rows; the evaluator refits exactly and records the verified budget $k$ and score $1-k/\\kcap$. Random, influence-only and opponent-targeted removal serve as comparators; empty-set, label-permutation and cross-table copy runs serve as controls. Development tables (Arena 55k, MT-Bench human) and held-out tables (Arena 140k deduplicated, NBA) are kept separate.}\n132\t\\label{fig:overview}\n133\t\\end{figure}\n134\t\n135\t\\section{Results}\n136\t\\subsection{Held-out removal budgets}\n137\tTable~\\ref{tab:cases} and Figure~\\ref{fig:main} give the verified budgets of the studied configuration on the four held-out cases. All four flipped within the cap (flip rate 1.0), and every one used a small share of its allowance: the family scores are 0.988 for crowd votes and 0.864 for sports, the overall score is 0.926, and the mean of $k/\\kcap$ over the four cases is 0.074. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics]]\n138\tIn the crowd table, deleting 62 of 135,630 rows (0.046\\%) reverses the top pair and 4 rows (0.003\\%) reverse the mid pair. In the sports table, 62 of 12,900 rows (0.48\\%) reverse the top pair and 8 rows (0.062\\%) reverse the mid pair. % @evidence[[derived/final_stats.json#cases.F1.studied_k]]\n139\tThe two episodes (seeds 21 and 22) produced identical budgets in every case, so we observe no episode-to-episode variation; with two episodes this is a consistency check on the search procedure, not an estimate of sampling variability. Table~\\ref{tab:agg} lists the aggregate held-out metrics next to the comparator references.\n140\t\n141\t\\begin{table}[t]\n142\t\\centering\n143\t\\caption{Aggregate held-out metrics (budget-saving score $1-k/\\kcap$, averaged over cases within a design, then over designs; the four real cases plus controls enter only through the control gap). Reference columns are the evaluator's score for random removal (B1) and influence-only removal (B2) aggregated the same way.}\n144\t\\label{tab:agg}\n145\t\\small\n146\t\\begin{tabular}{lr}\n147\t\\toprule\n148\tMetric & Value\\\\\n149\t\\midrule\n150\tOverall score (studied configuration) & 0.926\\\\\n151\t\\quad crowd family (F1, F2) & 0.988\\\\\n152\t\\quad sports family (F3, F4) & 0.864\\\\\n153\tReference score, random removal (B1) & 0.041\\\\\n154\tReference score, influence only (B2) & 0.926\\\\\n155\tFlip rate over real cases & 1.0\\\\\n156\tMean $k/\\kcap$ over real cases & 0.074\\\\\n157\tControl gap (permuted minus real, mean score) & $+0.112$\\\\\n158\t\\bottomrule\n159\t\\end{tabular}\n160\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics]]\n161\t\\end{table}\n162\t\n163\t\\begin{table}[t]\n164\t\\centering\n165\t\\caption{Held-out removal budgets. $k$ is the verified number of removed rows; percentages are $k/N$ (derived). B1 reports how many of 20 random orders flipped within the cap; B2 and B3 report the verified budget of each comparator. The studied configuration and B2 coincide in every case.}\n166\t\\label{tab:cases}\n167\t\\small\n168\t\\begin{tabular}{llrrrrrr}\n169\t\\toprule\n170\tCase & Pair & $N$ & $\\kcap$ & Studied $k$ (\\% of $N$) & B1 flips & B2 $k$ & B3 $k$\\\\\n171\t\\midrule\n172\tF1 & crowd, top & 135,630 & 2,713 & 62 (0.046\\%) & 0/20 & 62 & 650\\\\\n173\tF2 & crowd, mid & 135,630 & 2,713 & 4 (0.003\\%) & 6/20 & 4 & 6\\\\\n174\tF3 & NBA, top & 12,900 & 258 & 62 (0.48\\%) & 0/20 & 62 & 88\\\\\n175\tF4 & NBA, mid & 12,900 & 258 & 8 (0.062\\%) & 0/20 & 8 & 12\\\\\n176\t\\bottomrule\n177\t\\end{tabular}\n178\t% @evidence[[derived/final_stats.json#cases]] all cells of this table\n179\t\\end{table}\n180\t\n181\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n182\t\\centering\n183\t\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/main_budget_by_case.pdf}\n184\t\\caption{Verified removal budget as a percent of all comparisons (log scale) for the studied configuration, influence-only removal (B2) and opponent-targeted removal (B3), with the 2\\% cap shown. Numbers above bars are rows removed. Bars for the studied configuration and B2 coincide in all four cases. Below each category, the number of random orders (of 20) that flipped within the cap.}\n185\t\\label{fig:main}\n186\t\\end{figure}\n187\t\n188\t\\subsection{Top pairs versus mid pairs}\n189\tIn both tables the mid pair is cheaper than the top pair: 4 versus 62 rows in the crowd table and 8 versus 62 rows in the sports table. Random removal is informative here: it never flips a top pair (0 of 20 draws, in either table) or the sports mid pair, but flips the crowd mid pair in 6 of 20 draws. So the crowd mid pair is sufficiently close that undirected deletion of up to 2\\% of rows sometimes suffices, while an influence-ordered set needs only 4 rows. The registry does not record rating gaps, so we do not test whether smaller gaps explain this ordering.\n190\t\n191\t\\subsection{Crowd votes versus sports: a contrast with confounds}\\label{sec:contrast}\n192\tAs a share of comparisons the sports budgets are larger: the top-pair budget is 10.5 times and the mid-pair budget 21.0 times the crowd value (derived from $k/N$). % @evidence[[derived/final_stats.json#ratios]]\n193\tThe top-pair ratio is exactly the ratio of table sizes, $135{,}630/12{,}900=10.5$, because both top-pair budgets are 62 rows. The mid pairs differ by 4 versus 8 rows. Hence the per-share contrast is largely a denominator effect for the top pairs, and in absolute rows the two designs are equally fragile there. The designs also differ in participants (52 models versus 30 teams), era, outcome granularity, the tie structure, and the match graph. The choice of normaliser also matters. Dividing instead by the mean number of comparisons a participant appears in ($2N/M$, derived: 5,217 for the crowd table and 860 for the sports table) shrinks the contrast but does not remove it: the 62-row top-pair budget is 1.2\\% of a crowd participant's comparisons and 7.2\\% of a team's (about 6 times), and the mid-pair budgets are 0.077\\% and 0.93\\% (about 12 times), against 10.5 and 21.0 times under $k/N$. % @evidence[[derived/final_stats.json#cases.F1.studied_percent_of_mean_participant_comparisons]]\n194\tNeither normaliser is privileged: the share of the table suits a perturbation of a fixed fraction of all votes, the share of a participant's comparisons is closer to the evidence behind its rating. Both views give the same qualitative picture (small budgets, mid cheaper than top, sports the larger share), differing in the size of the ratio. We cannot separate these factors with two tables, and we therefore do not attribute the contrast to evaluation design. What the data do support is narrower: under one verified protocol, one adjacent top pair in each of two specific tables can be reversed by deleting 62 rows.\n195\t\n196\t\\subsection{The studied configuration equals influence-only removal}\n197\tThe studied configuration's budget equals that of B2 in all four held-out cases and in both permutation controls (Table~\\ref{tab:cases}, Supplement Table~\\ref{tab:full}), and the overall held-out score 0.926 equals the evaluator's reference score for B2 to the reported precision. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics]]\n198\tSo the re-linearisation, the leverage correction and the polish gave no measured reduction on these cases. The same holds on development tables (Section~\\ref{sec:dev}). A single static ranking by first-order influence followed by exact-refit bisection is therefore enough to reach every budget we report, and the budgets can be viewed as properties of that simple procedure's output rather than of the more elaborate one. Opponent-targeted removal needs more rows in all four cases: 650 versus 62, 6 versus 4, 88 versus 62 and 12 versus 8. The gap is large only for the crowd top pair (10.5 times), and modest elsewhere (1.4 to 1.5 times). Random removal's mean overall score is 0.041, against 0.926 for B2. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics]]\n199\t\n200\tWe did not run the starting baseline on the held-out tables. By inspection of its source, its first strategy is the same static influence ranking with prefix bisection as B2 and its second strategy runs only when the first finds no flip within the cap; we therefore expect, but have not measured, that it would reproduce B2's budgets here.\n201\t\n202\t\\subsection{Controls}\\label{sec:controls}\n203\tThe empty removal set flipped no case, and the cross-table copy control flipped 0 of 2 trials, which is consistent with the budgets not being artefacts of an already-reversed pair or of row position. The permutation controls were recorded as non-vacuous by the evaluator (Figure~\\ref{fig:controls}b): the fraction of moved labels was 0.660 against an expected 0.662 for the crowd table and 0.498 against 0.500 for the sports table, and the rating spread fell to 0.088 and 0.160 of the real value. % @evidence[[derived/final_stats.json#permutation_controls]]\n204\tThe top pair of a permuted table flipped after only 2 rows (crowd) and 10 rows (sports), compared with 62 rows on the real tables (Figure~\\ref{fig:controls}a); the mean score difference between permuted and real top pairs is $+0.112$. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.control_gap_permuted_minus_real]]\n205\tWith outcomes shuffled the ratings collapse together and a handful of rows reorders them. This does not show that real ranks are robust in any absolute sense, and the permuted tables are a lower reference, not a model of noise or manipulation.\n206\t\n207\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n208\t\\centering\n209\t\\includegraphics[width=0.75\\textwidth]{figures/controls_permutation.pdf}\n210\t\\caption{Label-permutation controls on the held-out tables. (a) Verified top-pair budget in rows (log scale) on the real table and on the label-permuted table. (b) Rating spread of the permuted fit relative to the real fit, with the evaluator's non-vacuity limit of 0.5.}\n211\t\\label{fig:controls}\n212\t\\end{figure}\n213\t\n214\t\\subsection{Development tables: a three-way tie and an unflipped expert table}\\label{sec:dev}\n215\tTable~\\ref{tab:dev} reports the development evaluation of three search configurations: the influence-guided configuration studied above (Configuration 1) and two further configurations that are identified in the evidence only by their titles, a cell-aggregated relaxed removal search (Configuration 2) and a stationarity-LP target-rating search (Configuration 3). All three scored 0.4993, the same as the starting baseline (difference 0.0); all three were scored (3 of 3, none missing). % @evidence[[paper_data.json#downstream_study]]\n216\tFor the studied configuration, the search-stage evaluator output splits this score into 0.9987 on the crowd family (Arena 55k) and 0.0 on the expert family (MT-Bench human), with an influence-only reference of 0.4993 and a random reference of 0.0695. These sub-scores are quoted from that logged evaluator output and have no entry in the evidence registry, which records only the three total scores and per-episode runtimes (about 1.3 s, 2.0 s and 114 s). We therefore give no breakdown for Configurations 2 and 3. Their source is not part of the staged inspectable evidence, so we make no statement about their algorithms beyond their titles; we mention them only because their total score did not exceed that of the starting baseline.\n217\t\n218\t\\begin{table}[t]\n219\t\\centering\n220\t\\caption{Development evaluation (Arena 55k and MT-Bench human; seeds 11 and 12). Scores are not comparable with held-out scores, which use different tables. $^\\dagger$Quoted from the logged search-stage evaluator output; no registry entry. Dashes: no grounded breakdown.}\n221\t\\label{tab:dev}\n222\t\\small\n223\t\\begin{tabular}{lrrrrr}\n224\t\\toprule\n225\tConfiguration & Score & Crowd & Expert & Flip rate & Time / episode (s)\\\\\n226\t\\midrule\n227\tConfiguration 1 (studied) & 0.4993 & 0.9987$^\\dagger$ & 0.0$^\\dagger$ & 0.5$^\\dagger$ & 1.3\\\\\n228\tConfiguration 2 & 0.4993 & -- & -- & -- & 2.0\\\\\n229\tConfiguration 3 & 0.4993 & -- & -- & -- & 114\\\\\n230\t\\midrule\n231\tStarting baseline & 0.4993 & \\multicolumn{3}{c}{not broken down} & --\\\\\n232\t\\bottomrule\n233\t\\end{tabular}\n234\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#all_programs]] scores and durations of the three configurations\n235\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#downstream_study]] baseline score\n236\t\\end{table}\n237\t\n238\tThe expert family score of 0.0 with a flip rate of 0.5 over the four non-control cases means that neither the top nor the mid pair of the six-model MT-Bench table flipped within a cap of 68 rows, while both crowd cases did. The two crowd cases together needed three rows on average over episodes (derived from the crowd score and the cap of 1,150). % @evidence[[paper_data.json#all_programs]] crowd score 0.998695..., cap from search_meta.json\n239\tWhether the expert table is inherently harder to reverse or the search was myopic cannot be determined: no exhaustive search was run on it, and no expert-design result exists on held-out data. The cause is unknown.\n240\t\n241\t\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n242\t\\paragraph{What the evidence supports.} Within the declared cases, adjacent-pair reversals under an exact BT refit can be certified with very small deletions: at most 62 rows from tables of 12,900 and 135,630 comparisons. The certified budgets are upper bounds. Far smaller sets could exist if a stronger search were available, so the figures should be read as ``at most this many rows'', and the finding that more elaborate search did not reduce them is a statement about the three configurations tried, not about the minimum.\n243\t\n244\t\\paragraph{What it does not support.} (i) \\emph{No causal claim about evaluation design.} Crowd votes and sports are confounded with table size, participants, era, outcome granularity and tie structure; one table per design means the contrast cannot be separated from table identity. (ii) \\emph{No claim about expert or model-judge designs on held-out data.} The only expert-design evidence is a six-model development table with no flip within the cap and an unknown cause; no model-judge data were evaluated. (iii) \\emph{No superiority claim.} The studied configuration equals influence-only removal in every case, and the starting baseline was not run on held-out tables. The held-out evaluation was recorded as qualified, but the qualification criteria are not recorded in the evidence, so this outcome neither establishes that the configuration is better nor extends findings beyond four cases. (iv) \\emph{No rank-uncertainty or manipulation claim.} A flip here is a reversal of a point-estimate order for one adjacent pair. We examine neither confidence-interval-based rank sets, nor insertion, label flipping or rigging by an adversary, nor other rating models; an adversary can usually do more than delete rows, and a platform's published rank may rest on other statistics. (v) \\emph{Limited uncertainty.} Two identical episodes, 20 random draws, and four cases do not support significance statements, and we compute no intervals over data sets.\n245\t\n246\t\\paragraph{Threats to validity.} The budget depends on the target pair; we study only the top two and the two middle ranks. The 2\\% cap truncates the search space; a reversal that would need more rows is reported as a failure and not as a budget. Permuted tables are a convenient null but not a model of realistic noise. Influence ordering uses a one-step Newton approximation. Ties count as half a win by evaluator convention. The development and held-out tables differ in release and preprocessing, and scores from the two scopes are never combined.\n247\t\n248\t\\paragraph{Implications for practice.} A one-shot influence ranking plus bisection over exact refits certifies small reversal budgets on tables of this size in seconds on a CPU. A reported adjacent order in these tables rests on few rows relative to the table; this does not say that the quality difference is small, nor that the order is wrong.\n249\t\n250\t\\section{Conclusion}\n251\tWe measured how few comparisons must be deleted to reverse the point-estimate order of one adjacent pair in a Bradley--Terry leaderboard, using exact-refit verification. On two held-out tables of real data, the verified budgets were 62 and 4 rows for the top and mid pairs of a 135,630-row crowd-vote table and 62 and 8 rows for a 12,900-row sports table. Mid pairs were cheaper than top pairs, and label-permuted tables flipped with far fewer rows than real ones. A static first-order influence ranking matched the more elaborate search everywhere, so we claim a measurement and not a better method. The crowd-versus-sports difference in percent of comparisons is largely table size and is confounded with participants and era. The expert-judgment development table did not flip within the cap, for an unknown reason. Natural next steps are more tables per design, rank-interval events, and exact solvers on small tables.\\label{lastbodypage}\n252\t\n253\t\\clearpage\n254\t\\bibliography{references}\n255\t\\bibliographystyle{paper}\n256\t\n257\t\\clearpage\n258\t\\appendix\n259\t\\section{Supplementary Information}\n260\t\n261\t\\subsection{Full per-case results including controls}\n262\tTable~\\ref{tab:full} lists all six held-out cases. The two seeded episodes (21 and 22) returned identical values in every cell. Evaluator-recorded durations per seeded episode were 4.45 s and 4.51 s.\n263\t\n264\t\\begin{table}[h]\n265\t\\centering\n266\t\\caption{All held-out cases. F5 and F6 are label-permutation controls of F1 and F3. Scores are $1-k/\\kcap$. B1 score is the mean over 20 random orders, with zero for a non-flipping order. Percent columns are $k/N$ (derived).}\n267\t\\label{tab:full}\n268\t\\footnotesize\n269\t\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{4pt}\n270\t\\begin{tabular}{lrrrrrrrrrr}\n271\t\\toprule\n272\tCase & $N$ & $\\kcap$ & $k$ & \\% & Score & B1 flips & B1 score & B2 $k$ & B3 $k$ & B3 score\\\\\n273\t\\midrule\n274\tF1 & 135,630 & 2,713 & 62 & 0.0457 & 0.977 & 0/20 & 0.000 & 62 & 650 & 0.760\\\\\n275\tF2 & 135,630 & 2,713 & 4 & 0.0029 & 0.999 & 6/20 & 0.164 & 4 & 6 & 0.998\\\\\n276\tF3 & 12,900 & 258 & 62 & 0.481 & 0.760 & 0/20 & 0.000 & 62 & 88 & 0.659\\\\\n277\tF4 & 12,900 & 258 & 8 & 0.062 & 0.969 & 0/20 & 0.000 & 8 & 12 & 0.953\\\\\n278\tF5 & 135,630 & 2,713 & 2 & 0.0015 & 0.999 & 7/20 & 0.251 & 2 & 2 & 0.999\\\\\n279\tF6 & 12,900 & 258 & 10 & 0.078 & 0.961 & 0/20 & 0.000 & 10 & 10 & 0.961\\\\\n280\t\\bottomrule\n281\t\\end{tabular}\n282\t% @evidence[[derived/final_stats.json#cases]] all cells of this table\n283\t\\end{table}\n284\t\n285\t\\subsection{Controls and qualification details}\n286\tThe empty-set control did not flip any case. The cross-table copy control recorded 0 flips in 2 trials; its construction is not documented in the staged evidence. The permutation controls report a moved-label fraction of 0.6601 (expected 0.6615) and a rating-spread ratio of 0.0882 for F5, and 0.4981 (expected 0.5000) and 0.1598 for F6, both recorded as non-vacuous by the evaluator. The mean score difference between permuted and real top pairs is $+0.112$. % @evidence[[derived/final_stats.json#permutation_controls]]\n287\tThe held-out evaluation completed with outcome qualified, and the recorded success indicator is true. The evidence does not record the gate criteria behind this outcome, so we do not interpret it as certifying that controls passed or that the evaluation is valid beyond the cases reported. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.outcome]]\n288\tDiagnostics volunteered by the candidate during evaluation are marked unverified by the evaluator and were not used for any number in this paper. Every reported budget comes from the evaluator's own exact refit.\n289\t\n290\t\\subsection{Development tables: additional detail}\n291\tBesides the scores in Table~\\ref{tab:dev}, the logged search-stage evaluator output for the studied configuration reports a random-removal reference of 0.0695, an influence-only reference of 0.4993, a mean budget fraction (of the cap, averaged over the four non-control cases) of 0.5007, and a permuted-minus-real control gap of 0.3153. These figures are quoted from that log and are not entries of the evidence registry. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#all_programs]] total score only\n292\tThe mean budget fraction is close to $0.5$ because the two expert cases that did not flip count as the full cap. Search scores and held-out scores belong to different evaluation scopes and tables; the near-identical magnitudes should not be compared. All three development configurations were scored (3 of 3, 0 unscored), so there is no missing development evidence.\n293\t\n294\t\\subsection{Algorithmic specification of the studied configuration}\n295\tThe Newton fit uses analytic gradient $\\nabla L=\\sum_i (p_i-s_i)(e_{a_i}-e_{b_i})+\\lambda\\theta$ and Hessian $H=\\sum_i w_i\\,x_ix_i^\\top+\\lambda I$, with step halving until the objective stops increasing (at most 60 iterations, gradient tolerance $10^{-9}$). Eq.~\\eqref{eq:infl} floors the leverage denominator $1-w_iq_i$ at $10^{-3}$. Step (b) uses a shrink factor $0.6$ and stops when the removed set reaches the current size limit. Step (c) begins at a drop fraction of $0.25$ and halves it after each failure. All fits are warm-started from the previous solution. The candidate's own acceptance test uses a margin of $10^{-5}$ on its own fit; how the evaluator's fit and acceptance test are implemented is not documented in the staged evidence. The candidate source reads the table as integer participant indices with a coded outcome, so model names are not used by it. Source: the selected candidate's \\texttt{src/program.py} in the staged evidence. A further file in that directory (\\texttt{datasets\\_offline.py}) holds loaders for offline analysis, was not imported by the evaluated code, and was not executed; we make no claim from it.\n296\t\n297\t\\subsection{Data provenance and reproduction}\n298\tDataset versions and licences, from the public task brief, are in Table~\\ref{tab:prov}. Raw data are not redistributed. The held-out tables are prepared by the evaluator; the evaluator metadata records the content-overlap removals and prompt-only overlaps reported in Section~\\ref{sec:data}, and we have no further documentation of its preparation or checking procedure. The figures are built by \\texttt{scripts/derive\\_final\\_stats.py} (derived percentages and ratios from the evaluator's recorded episodes) and \\texttt{scripts/build\\_figures.py} (Figures~\\ref{fig:main} and~\\ref{fig:controls}); Figure~\\ref{fig:overview} is a schematic with no quantitative content. Tables~\\ref{tab:cases} and~\\ref{tab:full} are filled from \\texttt{derived/final\\_stats.json}. The seeds are 11 and 12 (development) and 21 and 22 (held-out); Evaluating one candidate on the held-out tables takes seconds on a CPU (4.5 s per recorded episode here). We were not able to give independent timing for a full reproduction from raw data.\n299\t\n300\t\\begin{table}[h]\n301\t\\centering\n302\t\\caption{Dataset versions and licences as listed in the public task brief.}\n303\t\\label{tab:prov}\n304\t\\footnotesize\n305\t\\begin{tabular}{llll}\n306\t\\toprule\n307\tTable & Source & Version & Licence\\\\\n308\t\\midrule\n309\tArena 55k & \\texttt{lmarena-ai/arena-human-preference-55k} & commit \\texttt{18c29834} & apache-2.0\\\\\n310\tArena 140k & \\texttt{lmarena-ai/arena-human-preference-140k} & commit \\texttt{6322995a} & cc-by-4.0\\\\\n311\tMT-Bench human & \\texttt{lmsys/mt\\_bench\\_human\\_judgments} & commit \\texttt{f7d2896d} & cc-by-4.0\\\\\n312\tNBA Elo games & FiveThirtyEight \\texttt{nba-elo} & commit \\texttt{4c1ff5e3} & CC-BY-4.0\\\\\n313\t\\bottomrule\n314\t\\end{tabular}\n315\t\\end{table}\n316\t\n317\t\\subsection{LLM Usage}\n318\tLarge language models were used throughout this work. The search configurations and their code, the analysis scripts, and the text of this manuscript were produced with an LLM-based research assistant operating inside an automated pipeline that proposed configurations, ran a development evaluation, froze one selection, and then ran a sealed held-out evaluation whose results the manuscript reports. The assistant also drafted the related-work discussion from a small retrieved bibliography. Every figure and table in this paper is derived by script from the evaluator's recorded outputs. No claim in the paper relies on an LLM's unverified recollection of the literature; related work is described only at the level of the cited titles.\n319\t\n320\t\\subsection{Extended limitations}\n321\tFour held-out cases on two designs, two identical episodes and twenty random draws are the entire basis for the held-out claims. The development side adds one expert table of six participants. We did not run exhaustive or exact optimisation on any table, so we cannot say how far the certified budgets are from the minimum. We did not examine whether the reversed pairs are statistically distinguishable under sampling uncertainty, which would be the natural reading of an adjacent-pair flip for a practitioner. The bibliography available for this submission includes one work on vote rigging and one on influence functions but lacks the MT-Bench paper, classical case-deletion diagnostics and work on arena-platform practices beyond the platform description, so positioning relative to those lines is incomplete.\n322\t\n323\t\\end{document}\n324\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6c822923-d7cc-49c4-87fa-fe144f964a0b","uuid":"38eb6de5-f7e2-4e69-8414-46a339316add","timestamp":"2026-11-11T08:32:33.148Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/paper.tex","content":"\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Cases, seeds and reported uncertainty}\nEach table contributes two cases, a top pair and a mid pair, giving four held-out cases (crowd: F1 top, F2 mid; sports: F3 top, F4 mid) plus two permutation controls (F5, F6) on the top pairs. The held-out evaluation ran two seeded episodes (seeds 21 and 22); the development evaluation ran two (seeds 11 and 12). A family score is the mean of the case scores within a design, and the overall score is the mean of the two family scores. Uncertainty is limited by design: there are two episodes, B1 has 20 random draws per case, and the two held-out episodes returned identical per-case budgets. We therefore report no confidence intervals or significance tests, and nothing here is a population-level interval over data sets.\n\n\\subsection{Evaluation scopes and qualification}\nSearch configurations were scored on the development tables, and one frozen configuration was then evaluated in a single sealed held-out evaluation. We keep the two scopes in separate tables and sections and never combine their scores. No search configuration exceeded the starting baseline on the development tables (Section~\\ref{sec:dev}), so the frozen selection is not a winner; it is one of three configurations with identical development scores. Its held-out evaluation completed and was recorded as qualified, with a true success indicator. The evidence does not record which gate criteria the qualification decision covers, so we read it only as a statement that the sealed evaluation ran to completion and was recorded as qualified; we do not read it as certifying controls or validity. The control outcomes in Section~\\ref{sec:controls} are separate observations taken from the recorded episodes. Qualification is not evidence of superiority over the starting baseline, which was not run on the held-out tables, and it does not extend the findings beyond the declared cases.\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/protocol_overview.png}\n\\caption{Protocol overview. A comparison table is fit with a ridge-penalised Bradley--Terry model; a target adjacent pair (top or mid) is selected; a removal search proposes at most $2\\%$ of rows; the evaluator refits exactly and records the verified budget $k$ and score $1-k/\\kcap$. Random, influence-only and opponent-targeted removal serve as comparators; empty-set, label-permutation and cross-table copy runs serve as controls. Development tables (Arena 55k, MT-Bench human) and held-out tables (Arena 140k deduplicated, NBA) are kept separate.}\n\\label{fig:overview}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\section{Results}\n\\subsection{Held-out removal budgets}\nTable~\\ref{tab:cases} and Figure~\\ref{fig:main} give the verified budgets of the studied configuration on the four held-out cases. All four flipped within the cap (flip rate 1.0), and every one used a small share of its allowance: the family scores are 0.988 for crowd votes and 0.864 for sports, the overall score is 0.926, and the mean of $k/\\kcap$ over the four cases is 0.074. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics]]\nIn the crowd table, deleting 62 of 135,630 rows (0.046\\%) reverses the top pair and 4 rows (0.003\\%) reverse the mid pair. In the sports table, 62 of 12,900 rows (0.48\\%) reverse the top pair and 8 rows (0.062\\%) reverse the mid pair. % @evidence[[derived/final_stats.json#cases.F1.studied_k]]\nThe two episodes (seeds 21 and 22) produced identical budgets in every case, so we observe no episode-to-episode variation; with two episodes this is a consistency check on the search procedure, not an estimate of sampling variability. Table~\\ref{tab:agg} lists the aggregate held-out metrics next to the comparator references.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Aggregate held-out metrics (budget-saving score $1-k/\\kcap$, averaged over cases within a design, then over designs; the four real cases plus controls enter only through the control gap). Reference columns are the evaluator's score for random removal (B1) and influence-only removal (B2) aggregated the same way.}\n\\label{tab:agg}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lr}\n\\toprule\nMetric & Value\\\\\n\\midrule\nOverall score (studied configuration) & 0.926\\\\\n\\quad crowd family (F1, F2) & 0.988\\\\\n\\quad sports family (F3, F4) & 0.864\\\\\nReference score, random removal (B1) & 0.041\\\\\nReference score, influence only (B2) & 0.926\\\\\nFlip rate over real cases & 1.0\\\\\nMean $k/\\kcap$ over real cases & 0.074\\\\\nControl gap (permuted minus real, mean score) & $+0.112$\\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics]]\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Held-out removal budgets. $k$ is the verified number of removed rows; percentages are $k/N$ (derived). B1 reports how many of 20 random orders flipped within the cap; B2 and B3 report the verified budget of each comparator. The studied configuration and B2 coincide in every case.}\n\\label{tab:cases}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{llrrrrrr}\n\\toprule\nCase & Pair & $N$ & $\\kcap$ & Studied $k$ (\\% of $N$) & B1 flips & B2 $k$ & B3 $k$\\\\\n\\midrule\nF1 & crowd, top & 135,630 & 2,713 & 62 (0.046\\%) & 0/20 & 62 & 650\\\\\nF2 & crowd, mid & 135,630 & 2,713 & 4 (0.003\\%) & 6/20 & 4 & 6\\\\\nF3 & NBA, top & 12,900 & 258 & 62 (0.48\\%) & 0/20 & 62 & 88\\\\\nF4 & NBA, mid & 12,900 & 258 & 8 (0.062\\%) & 0/20 & 8 & 12\\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n% @evidence[[derived/final_stats.json#cases]] all cells of this table\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/main_budget_by_case.pdf}\n\\caption{Verified removal budget as a percent of all comparisons (log scale) for the studied configuration, influence-only removal (B2) and opponent-targeted removal (B3), with the 2\\% cap shown. Numbers above bars are rows removed. Bars for the studied configuration and B2 coincide in all four cases. Below each category, the number of random orders (of 20) that flipped within the cap.}\n\\label{fig:main}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\subsection{Top pairs versus mid pairs}\nIn both tables the mid pair is cheaper than the top pair: 4 versus 62 rows in the crowd table and 8 versus 62 rows in the sports table. Random removal is informative here: it never flips a top pair (0 of 20 draws, in either table) or the sports mid pair, but flips the crowd mid pair in 6 of 20 draws. So the crowd mid pair is sufficiently close that undirected deletion of up to 2\\% of rows sometimes suffices, while an influence-ordered set needs only 4 rows. The registry does not record rating gaps, so we do not test whether smaller gaps explain this ordering.\n\n\\subsection{Crowd votes versus sports: a contrast with confounds}\\label{sec:contrast}\nAs a share of comparisons the sports budgets are larger: the top-pair budget is 10.5 times and the mid-pair budget 21.0 times the crowd value (derived from $k/N$). % @evidence[[derived/final_stats.json#ratios]]\nThe top-pair ratio is exactly the ratio of table sizes, $135{,}630/12{,}900=10.5$, because both top-pair budgets are 62 rows. The mid pairs differ by 4 versus 8 rows. Hence the per-share contrast is largely a denominator effect for the top pairs, and in absolute rows the two designs are equally fragile there. The designs also differ in participants (52 models versus 30 teams), era, outcome granularity, the tie structure, and the match graph. The choice of normaliser also matters. Dividing instead by the mean number of comparisons a participant appears in ($2N/M$, derived: 5,217 for the crowd table and 860 for the sports table) shrinks the contrast but does not remove it: the 62-row top-pair budget is 1.2\\% of a crowd participant's comparisons and 7.2\\% of a team's (about 6 times), and the mid-pair budgets are 0.077\\% and 0.93\\% (about 12 times), against 10.5 and 21.0 times under $k/N$. % @evidence[[derived/final_stats.json#cases.F1.studied_percent_of_mean_participant_comparisons]]\nNeither normaliser is privileged: the share of the table suits a perturbation of a fixed fraction of all votes, the share of a participant's comparisons is closer to the evidence behind its rating. Both views give the same qualitative picture (small budgets, mid cheaper than top, sports the larger share), differing in the size of the ratio. We cannot separate these factors with two tables, and we therefore do not attribute the contrast to evaluation design. What the data do support is narrower: under one verified protocol, one adjacent top pair in each of two specific tables can be reversed by deleting 62 rows.\n\n\\subsection{The studied configuration equals influence-only removal}\nThe studied configuration's budget equals that of B2 in all four held-out cases and in both permutation controls (Table~\\ref{tab:cases}, Supplement Table~\\ref{tab:full}), and the overall held-out score 0.926 equals the evaluator's reference score for B2 to the reported precision. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics]]\nSo the re-linearisation, the leverage correction and the polish gave no measured reduction on these cases. The same holds on development tables (Section~\\ref{sec:dev}). A single static ranking by first-order influence followed by exact-refit bisection is therefore enough to reach every budget we report, and the budgets can be viewed as properties of that simple procedure's output rather than of the more elaborate one. Opponent-targeted removal needs more rows in all four cases: 650 versus 62, 6 versus 4, 88 versus 62 and 12 versus 8. The gap is large only for the crowd top pair (10.5 times), and modest elsewhere (1.4 to 1.5 times). Random removal's mean overall score is 0.041, against 0.926 for B2. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics]]\n\nWe did not run the starting baseline on the held-out tables. By inspection of its source, its first strategy is the same static influence ranking with prefix bisection as B2 and its second strategy runs only when the first finds no flip within the cap; we therefore expect, but have not measured, that it would reproduce B2's budgets here.\n\n\\subsection{Controls}\\label{sec:controls}\nThe empty removal set flipped no case, and the cross-table copy control flipped 0 of 2 trials, which is consistent with the budgets not being artefacts of an already-reversed pair or of row position. The permutation controls were recorded as non-vacuous by the evaluator (Figure~\\ref{fig:controls}b): the fraction of moved labels was 0.660 against an expected 0.662 for the crowd table and 0.498 against 0.500 for the sports table, and the rating spread fell to 0.088 and 0.160 of the real value. % @evidence[[derived/final_stats.json#permutation_controls]]\nThe top pair of a permuted table flipped after only 2 rows (crowd) and 10 rows (sports), compared with 62 rows on the real tables (Figure~\\ref{fig:controls}a); the mean score difference between permuted and real top pairs is $+0.112$. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.control_gap_permuted_minus_real]]\nWith outcomes shuffled the ratings collapse together and a handful of rows reorders them. This does not show that real ranks are robust in any absolute sense, and the permuted tables are a lower reference, not a model of noise or manipulation.\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=0.75\\textwidth]{figures/controls_permutation.pdf}\n\\caption{Label-permutation controls on the held-out tables. (a) Verified top-pair budget in rows (log scale) on the real table and on the label-permuted table. (b) Rating spread of the permuted fit relative to the real fit, with the evaluator's non-vacuity limit of 0.5.}\n\\label{fig:controls}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\subsection{Development tables: a three-way tie and an unflipped expert table}\\label{sec:dev}\nTable~\\ref{tab:dev} reports the development evaluation of three search configurations: the influence-guided configuration studied above (Configuration 1) and two further configurations that are identified in the evidence only by their titles, a cell-aggregated relaxed removal search (Configuration 2) and a stationarity-LP target-rating search (Configuration 3). All three scored 0.4993, the same as the starting baseline (difference 0.0); all three were scored (3 of 3, none missing). % @evidence[[paper_data.json#downstream_study]]\nFor the studied configuration, the search-stage evaluator output splits this score into 0.9987 on the crowd family (Arena 55k) and 0.0 on the expert family (MT-Bench human), with an influence-only reference of 0.4993 and a random reference of 0.0695. These sub-scores are quoted from that logged evaluator output and have no entry in the evidence registry, which records only the three total scores and per-episode runtimes (about 1.3 s, 2.0 s and 114 s). We therefore give no breakdown for Configurations 2 and 3. Their source is not part of the staged inspectable evidence, so we make no statement about their algorithms beyond their titles; we mention them only because their total score did not exceed that of the starting baseline.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Development evaluation (Arena 55k and MT-Bench human; seeds 11 and 12). Scores are not comparable with held-out scores, which use different tables. $^\\dagger$Quoted from the logged search-stage evaluator output; no registry entry. Dashes: no grounded breakdown.}\n\\label{tab:dev}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrrr}\n\\toprule\nConfiguration & Score & Crowd & Expert & Flip rate & Time / episode (s)\\\\\n\\midrule\nConfiguration 1 (studied) & 0.4993 & 0.9987$^\\dagger$ & 0.0$^\\dagger$ & 0.5$^\\dagger$ & 1.3\\\\\nConfiguration 2 & 0.4993 & -- & -- & -- & 2.0\\\\\nConfiguration 3 & 0.4993 & -- & -- & -- & 114\\\\\n\\midrule\nStarting baseline & 0.4993 & \\multicolumn{3}{c}{not broken down} & --\\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#all_programs]] scores and durations of the three configurations\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#downstream_study]] baseline score\n\\end{table}\n\nThe expert family score of 0.0 with a flip rate of 0.5 over the four non-control cases means that neither the top nor the mid pair of the six-model MT-Bench table flipped within a cap of 68 rows, while both crowd cases did. The two crowd cases together needed three rows on average over episodes (derived from the crowd score and the cap of 1,150). % @evidence[[paper_data.json#all_programs]] crowd score 0.998695..., cap from search_meta.json\nWhether the expert table is inherently harder to reverse or the search was myopic cannot be determined: no exhaustive search was run on it, and no expert-design result exists on held-out data. The cause is unknown.\n\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n\\paragraph{What the evidence supports.} Within the declared cases, adjacent-pair reversals under an exact BT refit can be certified with very small deletions: at most 62 rows from tables of 12,900 and 135,630 comparisons. The certified budgets are upper bounds. Far smaller sets could exist if a stronger search were available, so the figures should be read as ``at most this many rows'', and the finding that more elaborate search did not reduce them is a statement about the three configurations tried, not about the minimum.\n\n\\paragraph{What it does not support.} (i) \\emph{No causal claim about evaluation design.} Crowd votes and sports are confounded with table size, participants, era, outcome granularity and tie structure; one table per design means the contrast cannot be separated from table identity. (ii) \\emph{No claim about expert or model-judge designs on held-out data.} The only expert-design evidence is a six-model development table with no flip within the cap and an unknown cause; no model-judge data were evaluated. (iii) \\emph{No superiority claim.} The studied configuration equals influence-only removal in every case, and the starting baseline was not run on held-out tables. The held-out evaluation was recorded as qualified, but the qualification criteria are not recorded in the evidence, so this outcome neither establishes that the configuration is better nor extends findings beyond four cases. (iv) \\emph{No rank-uncertainty or manipulation claim.} A flip here is a reversal of a point-estimate order for one adjacent pair. We examine neither confidence-interval-based rank sets, nor insertion, label flipping or rigging by an adversary, nor other rating models; an adversary can usually do more than delete rows, and a platform's published rank may rest on other statistics. (v) \\emph{Limited uncertainty.} Two identical episodes, 20 random draws, and four cases do not support significance statements, and we compute no intervals over data sets.\n\n\\paragraph{Threats to validity.} The budget depends on the target pair; we study only the top two and the two middle ranks. The 2\\% cap truncates the search space; a reversal that would need more rows is reported as a failure and not as a budget. Permuted tables are a convenient null but not a model of realistic noise. Influence ordering uses a one-step Newton approximation. Ties count as half a win by evaluator convention. The development and held-out tables differ in release and preprocessing, and scores from the two scopes are never combined.\n\n\\paragraph{Implications for practice.} A one-shot influence ranking plus bisection over exact refits certifies small reversal budgets on tables of this size in seconds on a CPU. A reported adjacent order in these tables rests on few rows relative to the table; this does not say that the quality difference is small, nor that the order is wrong.\n\n\\section{Conclusion}\nWe measured how few comparisons must be deleted to reverse the point-estimate order of one adjacent pair in a Bradley--Terry leaderboard, using exact-refit verification. On two held-out tables of real data, the verified budgets were 62 and 4 rows for the top and mid pairs of a 135,630-row crowd-vote table and 62 and 8 rows for a 12,900-row sports table. Mid pairs were cheaper than top pairs, and label-permuted tables flipped with far fewer rows than real ones. A static first-order influence ranking matched the more elaborate search everywhere, so we claim a measurement and not a better method. The crowd-versus-sports difference in percent of comparisons is largely table size and is confounded with participants and era. The expert-judgment development table did not flip within the cap, for an unknown reason. Natural next steps are more tables per design, rank-interval events, and exact solvers on small tables.\\label{lastbodypage}\n\n\\clearpage\n\\bibliography{references}\n\\bibliographystyle{paper}\n\n\\clearpage\n\\appendix\n\\section{Supplementary Information}\n\n\\subsection{Full per-case results including controls}\nTable~\\ref{tab:full} lists all six held-out cases. The two seeded episodes (21 and 22) returned identical values in every cell. Evaluator-recorded durations per seeded episode were 4.45 s and 4.51 s.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{All held-out cases. F5 and F6 are label-permutation controls of F1 and F3. Scores are $1-k/\\kcap$. B1 score is the mean over 20 random orders, with zero for a non-flipping order. Percent columns are $k/N$ (derived).}\n\\label{tab:full}\n\\footnotesize\n\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{4pt}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrrrrrrrr}\n\\toprule\nCase & $N$ & $\\kcap$ & $k$ & \\% & Score & B1 flips & B1 score & B2 $k$ & B3 $k$ & B3 score\\\\\n\\midrule\nF1 & 135,630 & 2,713 & 62 & 0.0457 & 0.977 & 0/20 & 0.000 & 62 & 650 & 0.760\\\\\nF2 & 135,630 & 2,713 & 4 & 0.0029 & 0.999 & 6/20 & 0.164 & 4 & 6 & 0.998\\\\\nF3 & 12,900 & 258 & 62 & 0.481 & 0.760 & 0/20 & 0.000 & 62 & 88 & 0.659\\\\\nF4 & 12,900 & 258 & 8 & 0.062 & 0.969 & 0/20 & 0.000 & 8 & 12 & 0.953\\\\\nF5 & 135,630 & 2,713 & 2 & 0.0015 & 0.999 & 7/20 & 0.251 & 2 & 2 & 0.999\\\\\nF6 & 12,900 & 258 & 10 & 0.078 & 0.961 & 0/20 & 0.000 & 10 & 10 & 0.961\\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n% @evidence[[derived/final_stats.json#cases]] all cells of this table\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Controls and qualification details}\nThe empty-set control did not flip any case. The cross-table copy control recorded 0 flips in 2 trials; its construction is not documented in the staged evidence. The permutation controls report a moved-label fraction of 0.6601 (expected 0.6615) and a rating-spread ratio of 0.0882 for F5, and 0.4981 (expected 0.5000) and 0.1598 for F6, both recorded as non-vacuous by the evaluator. The mean score difference between permuted and real top pairs is $+0.112$. % @evidence[[derived/final_stats.json#permutation_controls]]\nThe held-out evaluation completed with outcome qualified, and the recorded success indicator is true. The evidence does not record the gate criteria behind this outcome, so we do not interpret it as certifying that controls passed or that the evaluation is valid beyond the cases reported. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.outcome]]\nDiagnostics volunteered by the candidate during evaluation are marked unverified by the evaluator and were not used for any number in this paper. Every reported budget comes from the evaluator's own exact refit.\n\n\\subsection{Development tables: additional detail}\nBesides the scores in Table~\\ref{tab:dev}, the logged search-stage evaluator output for the studied configuration reports a random-removal reference of 0.0695, an influence-only reference of 0.4993, a mean budget fraction (of the cap, averaged over the four non-control cases) of 0.5007, and a permuted-minus-real control gap of 0.3153. These figures are quoted from that log and are not entries of the evidence registry. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#all_programs]] total score only\nThe mean budget fraction is close to $0.5$ because the two expert cases that did not flip count as the full cap. Search scores and held-out scores belong to different evaluation scopes and tables; the near-identical magnitudes should not be compared. All three development configurations were scored (3 of 3, 0 unscored), so there is no missing development evidence.\n\n\\subsection{Algorithmic specification of the studied configuration}\nThe Newton fit uses analytic gradient $\\nabla L=\\sum_i (p_i-s_i)(e_{a_i}-e_{b_i})+\\lambda\\theta$ and Hessian $H=\\sum_i w_i\\,x_ix_i^\\top+\\lambda I$, with step halving until the objective stops increasing (at most 60 iterations, gradient tolerance $10^{-9}$). Eq.~\\eqref{eq:infl} floors the leverage denominator $1-w_iq_i$ at $10^{-3}$. Step (b) uses a shrink factor $0.6$ and stops when the removed set reaches the current size limit. Step (c) begins at a drop fraction of $0.25$ and halves it after each failure. All fits are warm-started from the previous solution. The candidate's own acceptance test uses a margin of $10^{-5}$ on its own fit; how the evaluator's fit and acceptance test are implemented is not documented in the staged evidence. The candidate source reads the table as integer participant indices with a coded outcome, so model names are not used by it. Source: the selected candidate's \\texttt{src/program.py} in the staged evidence. A further file in that directory (\\texttt{datasets\\_offline.py}) holds loaders for offline analysis, was not imported by the evaluated code, and was not executed; we make no claim from it.\n\n\\subsection{Data provenance and reproduction}\nDataset versions and licences, from the public task brief, are in Table~\\ref{tab:prov}. Raw data are not redistributed. The held-out tables are prepared by the evaluator; the evaluator metadata records the content-overlap removals and prompt-only overlaps reported in Section~\\ref{sec:data}, and we have no further documentation of its preparation or checking procedure. The figures are built by \\texttt{scripts/derive\\_final\\_stats.py} (derived percentages and ratios from the evaluator's recorded episodes) and \\texttt{scripts/build\\_figures.py} (Figures~\\ref{fig:main} and~\\ref{fig:controls}); Figure~\\ref{fig:overview} is a schematic with no quantitative content. Tables~\\ref{tab:cases} and~\\ref{tab:full} are filled from \\texttt{derived/final\\_stats.json}. The seeds are 11 and 12 (development) and 21 and 22 (held-out); Evaluating one candidate on the held-out tables takes seconds on a CPU (4.5 s per recorded episode here). We were not able to give independent timing for a full reproduction from raw data.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Dataset versions and licences as listed in the public task brief.}\n\\label{tab:prov}\n\\footnotesize\n\\begin{tabular}{llll}\n\\toprule\nTable & Source & Version & Licence\\\\\n\\midrule\nArena 55k & \\texttt{lmarena-ai/arena-human-preference-55k} & commit \\texttt{18c29834} & apache-2.0\\\\\nArena 140k & \\texttt{lmarena-ai/arena-human-preference-140k} & commit \\texttt{6322995a} & cc-by-4.0\\\\\nMT-Bench human & \\texttt{lmsys/mt\\_bench\\_human\\_judgments} & commit \\texttt{f7d2896d} & cc-by-4.0\\\\\nNBA Elo games & FiveThirtyEight \\texttt{nba-elo} & commit \\texttt{4c1ff5e3} & CC-BY-4.0\\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{LLM Usage}\nLarge language models were used throughout this work. The search configurations and their code, the analysis scripts, and the text of this manuscript were produced with an LLM-based research assistant operating inside an automated pipeline that proposed configurations, ran a development evaluation, froze one selection, and then ran a sealed held-out evaluation whose results the manuscript reports. The assistant also drafted the related-work discussion from a small retrieved bibliography. Every figure and table in this paper is derived by script from the evaluator's recorded outputs. No claim in the paper relies on an LLM's unverified recollection of the literature; related work is described only at the level of the cited titles.\n\n\\subsection{Extended limitations}\nFour held-out cases on two designs, two identical episodes and twenty random draws are the entire basis for the held-out claims. The development side adds one expert table of six participants. We did not run exhaustive or exact optimisation on any table, so we cannot say how far the certified budgets are from the minimum. We did not examine whether the reversed pairs are statistically distinguishable under sampling uncertainty, which would be the natural reading of an adjacent-pair flip for a practitioner. The bibliography available for this submission includes one work on vote rigging and one on influence functions but lacks the MT-Bench paper, classical case-deletion diagnostics and work on arena-platform practices beyond the platform description, so positioning relative to those lines is incomplete.\n\n\\end{document}\n","numLines":205,"startLine":120,"totalLines":324}}}
